WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.908
Storyboarding and Rapid Prototyping.

2
00:00:03.908 --> 00:00:08.249
ID 553 - Module 6: Creating Instruction.

3
00:00:08.249 --> 00:00:14.762
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.362 --> 00:00:18.220
A design document describes instruction.

5
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.936
A prototype lets people experience it.

6
00:00:20.936 --> 00:00:31.796
Stakeholders who approve a document often reject the module
built from it, because the document never made them imagine
what learners would actually do.

7
00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:36.441
Prototypes surface those reactions early, when changes are
cheap.

8
00:00:36.441 --> 00:00:49.088
They also expose problems the designer can't see from the
inside: an interaction that's confusing, a scenario that
feels fake, a page that takes three times longer than
planned.

9
00:00:49.088 --> 00:00:57.520
Prototyping is the core of SAM, and it fits inside ADDIE and
Dick and Carey as the front half of formative evaluation.

10
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.638
Let's follow one interaction through four levels of
fidelity.

11
00:01:02.638 --> 00:01:12.564
The interaction: a mismatch alert appears during medication
administration, and the learner must choose to stop,
override, or proceed.

12
00:01:12.564 --> 00:01:20.119
At paper fidelity, that's three index cards, one per choice,
with the consequence written on the back.

13
00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:25.008
You read the scenario aloud, the tester picks a card and
flips it.

14
00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:26.711
Twenty minutes to make.

15
00:01:26.711 --> 00:01:35.452
It tests whether the sequence makes sense, whether the
scenario is plausible, and whether the consequences feel
right.

16
00:01:35.452 --> 00:01:41.748
In my first test, a nurse said the override option would
never be offered that early.

17
00:01:41.748 --> 00:01:45.007
The cards changed before anything was built.

18
00:01:45.607 --> 00:01:54.301
Low fidelity is clickable slides: placeholder graphics, the
three choices as buttons linking to consequence slides.

19
00:01:54.301 --> 00:01:55.208
A few hours.

20
00:01:55.208 --> 00:01:59.896
It tests the interaction logic and the wording of the
choices.

21
00:01:59.896 --> 00:02:12.143
Medium fidelity is an authoring-tool build with real text,
draft graphics, and synthetic narration; a few days, and it
tests look and feel, timing, and usability.

22
00:02:12.143 --> 00:02:20.988
High fidelity is near-final with produced media, and tests
polish, technical function, and accessibility conformance.

23
00:02:20.988 --> 00:02:32.329
The rule: start at the lowest fidelity that can answer your
current question, and move up only when the lower version
stops producing useful findings.

24
00:02:32.329 --> 00:02:37.999
Test the flow on paper before you write a single line in an
authoring tool.

25
00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:45.704
A storyboard describes each screen: what the learner sees,
hears, and does, and what happens next.

26
00:02:45.704 --> 00:02:52.447
It's the handoff between designer and developer and the main
artifact for stakeholder review.

27
00:02:52.447 --> 00:03:19.997
Each row carries a screen ID tied to the lesson and
objective, the Gagne event the screen serves, the visual,
the exact on-screen text, the narration script with a note
on whether it replaces or supplements text, the interaction
and branching rules, the feedback for each response,
navigation, accessibility notes such as alt text and
keyboard alternatives, and development notes.

28
00:03:19.997 --> 00:03:23.623
A twenty-minute module runs fifteen to forty rows.

29
00:03:23.623 --> 00:03:27.030
A table in a document or spreadsheet is enough.

30
00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:32.768
Decision practice is where most e-learning fails to go
beyond click-next.

31
00:03:32.768 --> 00:03:40.369
A branching scenario presents a situation, asks the learner
to decide, shows the consequence, and continues.

32
00:03:40.369 --> 00:03:52.194
Design it from your task analysis: every branch point in the
procedural analysis becomes a decision screen, and the
common errors you recorded become the wrong options.

33
00:03:52.194 --> 00:03:59.373
Keep it manageable: three or four decisions with two or
three options each and a few converging paths.

34
00:03:59.373 --> 00:04:03.174
Prototype it in Twine or on cards before you build it.

35
00:04:03.774 --> 00:04:09.584
Even a paper prototype should be tested with someone who
resembles the learners.

36
00:04:09.584 --> 00:04:13.433
A one-to-one test takes thirty to forty-five minutes.

37
00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:17.427
Explain that you're testing the design, not the person.

38
00:04:17.427 --> 00:04:21.421
Ask them to work through the prototype, thinking aloud.

39
00:04:21.421 --> 00:04:27.159
Don't help unless they're stuck for more than a minute, and
note where and why.

40
00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:36.818
Afterward, ask what was confusing, what was too easy or too
hard, what they'd change, and whether they can perform the
objective now.

41
00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:41.902
Record the findings and decide what changes before the next
iteration.

42
00:04:41.902 --> 00:04:46.041
Three one-to-one tests find most of the serious problems.

43
00:04:46.641 --> 00:04:50.688
Most designers build content with a subject-matter expert.

44
00:04:50.688 --> 00:04:58.991
Give them a specific task with a deadline: review this
storyboard for accuracy, or provide three realistic error
cases.

45
00:04:58.991 --> 00:05:06.805
Explain your design decisions in terms of learner
performance so they understand why the module isn't a
lecture.

46
00:05:06.805 --> 00:05:12.526
Separate accuracy review, which is their call, from design
review, which is yours.

47
00:05:12.526 --> 00:05:17.968
And prototype early so they react to something concrete
instead of a document.

48
00:05:17.968 --> 00:05:27.108
For assignment seven, you'll produce a complete storyboard
for one lesson and a low-fidelity prototype of at least one
interaction.

49
00:05:27.108 --> 00:05:34.992
Choose the lesson that best represents your strategy and the
interaction that carries the most decision practice.
